=== DNA-guided CRISPR ===
{{main|DNA-guided CRISPR}}
Originally introduced by the Jain lab in pre-print, modified versions of Class II systems (specifically Cas12a) function with an artificial single-stranded DNA guide in place of natural RNA.<ref name="Orosco_medRxiv_2024">{{cite journal | vauthors = Orosco C, Rananaware SR, Huang B, Hanna MP, Ahmadimashhadi MR, Lewis JG, Baugh MP, Bodin AP, Flannery SJ, Lange IH, Fang ZR, Karalkar VN, Meister KS, Jain PK | title = DNA-guided CRISPR/Cas12 for RNA targeting | journal = medRxiv [Preprint] | date = 22 November 2024 | doi = 10.1101/2024.11.21.24317744 }}</ref> Within this new paradigm, the system's endonuclease activity now is redirected towards cleaving ssRNA instead of dsDNA.<ref name="Orosco_NatureBiotech_2026">{{cite journal | vauthors = Orosco C, Huang B, Rananaware SR, Bodin AP, Browning I, Fang A, Baugh MP, Lange IH, Elhabashy YB, Balaraju M, Lewis JG, Shah NH, Hanna MP, Flannery SJ, Meister KS, Karalkar V, Jain PK | title = DNA-guided CRISPR/Cas12 for RNA targeting | journal = Nature Biotechnology | year = 2026 | pages = 1–12 | doi = 10.1038/s41587-026-03129-w | pmid = 42141118 }}</ref><ref name="Structural_basis_2026">{{cite journal | title = Structural basis of DNA-guided RNA targeting | journal = bioRxiv | date = 19 March 2026 | doi = 10.64898/2026.03.19.712971 }}</ref> With DNA as the guide, the need for intricate RNA preparation and preservation is no longer required, allowing Class II effectors more versatility in programmable RNA targeting and translational biotechnology.<ref name="Orosco_NatureBiotech_2026" /><ref name="Wiegel_2026">{{cite news | last = Wiegel | first = Savannah | title = DNA-guided CRISPR Suggests a New Direction for RNA Editing | work = Genetic Engineering & Biotechnology News | url= https://www.genengnews.com/topics/genome-editing/dna%E2%80%91guided-crispr-suggests-a-new-direction-for-rna-editing/}}</ref> 

